{"kind":"request","text":"codex: hugh has asked for a change to the MCP adapter strategy, and settled the design frame. Today gitseq-mcp binds actor at startup (--actor) and defaults repo to its working dir; running one agent harness across multiple identities/repos forces one adapter process per identity (this session runs gitseq as claude and gitseq-as-planner as planner for exactly that reason). Make repo AND agent per-call parameters so one adapter can serve many. Hugh's framing, which is the crux: the real trust boundary is key access; (repo, actor) is just the best shorthand for which held signing key to use — better than a filepath because it is stable, human-meaningful, and matches the identifiers the durable log already uses. So this is an ergonomics/plumbing change, not a trust-model change, PROVIDED the parameter selects a key the process can access rather than asserting an identity. Please design and implement on that basis, or come back with a better one.","body":{"conditions":"Add per-call repo and agent (actor) parameters to the gitseq-mcp tool surface. FRAME: the trust boundary IS key access; (repo, actor) is a SELECTOR for a signing key the adapter process can already access, never an assertion that mints or grants identity. Requirements: (1) Both parameters OPTIONAL with startup defaults — repo defaults to the adapter's working dir (repo-forwarding already exists; extend/confirm it end-to-end), agent defaults to the startup --actor — so existing single-identity adapters are unchanged and multi-identity harnesses opt in. (2) SELECTOR, NOT MINTER: resolve (repo, actor) to an EXISTING accessible key; never derive or create a key on demand for an arbitrary actor name (deriving-on-name is what would turn the shorthand back into forgery). (3) REFUSE, DO NOT FALL BACK: if the named actor has no accessible key, or the repo is unavailable, the call must hard-error — never silently sign as the startup default. Silent wrong-identity signing is the specific failure this must preclude. (4) Resolve and validate the actor against the resident's roster and reject unknown actors (the MCP roster is otherwise frozen at startup). (5) Document the trust assumption explicitly in the adapter docs and SKILL.md: the boundary is key access, and note that today's dev key model derives keys from the actor name (so the shorthand does not yet gate — it becomes a real boundary only once keys are access-gated secrets); a future maintainer must not read this change as adding a boundary it does not. Tests: per-call repo selects the right workroom; per-call agent signs as the selected actor when its key is accessible; a name with NO accessible key REFUSES rather than falling back; an unknown-to-roster actor refuses; defaults preserve current single-adapter behaviour. Layer: cmd/gitseq-mcp application/CLI surface; no kernel/fold change. Exact clean pushed head, full gates, per-path artifacts, independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review. Do not merge. If you judge a different shorthand or key-resolution model better, propose it back rather than implementing silently.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}